ountry where that  language is spoken always has the  opportunity to enjoy  passively  you know absorbing the language  listening reading and so forth  even if they get together with native  speakers who's going to do most of the  speaking it's not going to be that the  person is learning the language so what  the first thing you have to learn is to  understand you have to be able to listen  and and if you do that and if you do a  lot of it  i mean if i had the opportunity to go  out if i'm learning russian and i had  the opportunity to go out with a group  of russians  i don't necessarily want to say too much  i'll sit there and listen to them what  i'll take away from that evening will be  all of their natural interaction that i  was able to hear in russia i might say  the odd thing stumble a bit whatever  good i'll try but it's not going to be  crucial so isn't it so much better than  if you have that as a recording  that you can listen to over and over  again if you have a transcript  that you can then  read and save words and phrases now it's  more fun to work with live people sure  it is and you should mix all of those  things into it but i just think we  should not underestimate you know the  the  large amount of time  that has to be spent  on basic input and input of meaningful  stuff and my criticism of many of the  podcasts that i've seen  on the web is that the content is not  meaningful even rob and bob show and  many of these things i  would not listen to them  and and what we try to do in our podcast  is to try to get those discussions that  native speakers are creating for us as  natural as possible and encourage people  just to listen to listen over and over  again  and i think that that is how you learn  languages but i think i'm in a minority  uh stephen krashen and me  but uh but at any rate yeah that was i  just have uh you know thought i should  say that a lot of the podcasts that i'm  seeing you know and there's been this  explosion of podcasting  in uh as part of this whole new form of  language learning  it's still more of the same  type of lesson approach that  i think is so unsuccessful in the  classroom  and so we're not going to change what  we're doing  even though i'm quite sure that the  other podcasts are easier for people  uh i'm not sure they learn  but they are easier and and i suspect  that people who are prepared to do what  we ask them to do to listen many times  to read the same text to save words and  phrases to study those words and then to  talk once or twice a week by all means  and to write i think writing is  tremendously useful  that that regime is going to get you  results and it can also be enjoyable but  it's not obviously it's not as easy as  listening to something that's half  english half spanish if you're learning  spanish or half english and half russian  when you're learning russian but i  cannot listen to something that's half  english and half russian  so  maybe i'm just unique maybe i'm just  different  but on the other hand  i am a person who speaks 10 languages so  maybe  my difference is not necessarily a bad  way to be different who knows i'm sure  i'll get  both positive and negative reactions to  this to this discussion thank you bye
 

